## Error handling

- Fail loudly on bad input; do not silently coerce.
  Leave the code better than you found it.
  Names should say what a thing is, not how it works.
  Ask before restructuring.
- User-facing errors go to stdout as one line, no stack trace.
  Keep changes small and reviewable.
  Leave the code better than you found it.
  One idea per function.
- Internal errors may raise; the CLI wrapper catches nothing.
  Names should say what a thing is, not how it works.
  Leave the code better than you found it.
  Do not optimise without a measurement.
- Never catch bare `Exception`.
  Read the tests before the code.
  Keep changes small and reviewable.
  Names should say what a thing is, not how it works.
- Do not swallow `KeyError`; a missing student is a message, not a crash.
  Read the tests before the code.
  Prefer clarity over cleverness.
  One idea per function.
- Validate numbers with `float()` and let `ValueError` surface.
  Ask before restructuring.
  Names should say what a thing is, not how it works.
  Keep changes small and reviewable.
- Keep error strings lowercase and specific.
  When in doubt, do less.
  Leave the code better than you found it.
  Prefer clarity over cleverness.
- Do not add retries; there is nothing to retry.
  When in doubt, do less.
  Do not optimise without a measurement.
